  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nHigh pressure continues to flex its muscle over the eastern CONUS \nearly this morning. Water vapor shows fleeting Pacific moisture \nreturn to a cold front working its way through the Midwest, but \nbattling the aformentioned high. Satellite and radar show a broken \nline of showers from central WI (whose population is basking in a \nWorld Series championship) through eastern Iowa, while a better \norganized area of rain occurs north-northeast of there. Back at \nhome, preexisting dry air keeps a majority of the day dry. However, \nthere is growing concensus that modest saturation below 16,000 ft \nand adequate upper-level forcing from the right entrance region of a \ndeparting 500-mb jet will be enough for at least isoalted showers \nalong the cold front from west to east after 12pm EDT. Cannot \ncompletely rule out an isoalted thunderstorm as a thin ribbon of \ninstability accompanies the front, per high resolution ensembles. \nSkies quickly clear out behind the front and lows will tumble to \nnear 40 by Friday morning. \n\nThe rest of the forecast period is characterized by cooler, but \nstill above-normal temperatures, and increasing chances for \nrain. We continue to monitor the 20-40% chance of showers \nSaturday west of I- 69 as a weak shortwave lifts in from the \nSouthern Plains. Preceding high pressure, will be fighting to \nkeep Halloween dry, yet the rain chance must continue at this \ntime. The better surge of moisture arrives in the Sunday-Monday \ntime frame as high pressure centers over the Appalachians \nsteering Gulf moisture poleward. This area of rain would be \nfollowed by a mid-latitude cyclone in the Tuesday- Wednesday \nperiod, bringing mild and breezy conditions ahead of it, rain, \nand then a dose of cooler air. The magnitude of these details \nremain murky owing to the uncertain degree of phasing between a \nsubtropical jet moving over the Baja, and a Great Basin trough \npoised to move onshore this weekend.",
